SUMMARY:After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics
DESCRIPTION:After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics                                                                           \n        \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n	\n\n	\n\n\n			\nEvents\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    \n\nOctober 9\, 2026 – April 25\, 2027\nExhibition Hall. Third Floor\nIncluded with admission. Free for Gardiner Members\n\n\n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n  Get Tickets\n\n\n\n  Become a Member\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    The Gardiner Museum\, Black Artists’ Networks in Dialogue (BAND)\, and the Museum of the African Diaspora (MoAD) present a landmark curatorial partnership exploring contemporary ceramic practices rooted in Caribbean histories\, memory\, and cultural transformation. \nPresented across two exhibitions and two cities—Toronto and San Francisco—this collaboration brings together artists whose work examines migration\, ornament\, labour\, ancestry\, and the enduring impact of Caribbean cultural traditions across generations and geographies. \nAt the Gardiner Museum\, After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics considers how artists of the Caribbean diaspora are embracing the medium of ceramics to explore themes of knowledge transfer and the liberatory potential of the clandestine. Through existing and new work by Caribbean Canadian artists PJ Anderson\, Eddy Firmin\, Manuel Mathieu\, and Sharon Norwood\, the exhibition approaches questions of identity\, belonging\, memory\, and cultural continuity through the language of clay. \nAt MoAD\, After the Island: Ornament and Ceramic Practice in the Jamaican Diaspora focuses on artists whose practices engage Jamaica as memory\, inheritance\, orientation\, and cultural touchstone. The exhibition examines how ornament functions as a carrier of history\, labour\, ancestral knowledge\, and relation across time and distance. \nTogether\, After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics and After the Island: Ornament and Ceramic Practice in the Jamaican Diaspora offer a timely exploration of migration\, memory\, and cultural continuity\, demonstrating how artists working with clay continue to create new forms of relation between past and present\, homeland and diaspora\, history and imagination. \nThroughout both presentations\, vessels\, decorative forms\, sculptural installations\, and ceramic surfaces become sites where histories are preserved\, challenged\, reimagined\, and carried forward. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Exhibition Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Presenting Sponsors\n                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Supporting Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Government Support\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Project Partner\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Media Partners\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Thanks To\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Image: Eddy Firmin\, Sans signe\, 2023\, Ceramic\, wood\, fabric\, straw\, Variable dimensions. Photo: Michael Patten \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/after-the-island-caribbean-canadian-ceramics/
LOCATION:Gardiner Museum\, 111 Queen's Park\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S 2C7\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
